  Reading it. In LSB the TC=0 drift appears to RISE towards 70 %,
  which looks like temperature sensitivity but is not: arbiter noise
  is a fixed number of ps, while the LSB shrinks with the silicon, so
  the same jitter simply spans more codes. In ps the TC=0 drift is
  flat at roughly the arbiter sigma across the whole sweep - that is
  the ratiometric cancellation, and it is the column to quote.
  The TC term is the DIFFERENCE above: near zero at 100 % and growing
  symmetrically with |scale-100|, as a mismatch model predicts.
  Caveat: the uniform scaling cancels by construction in this model,
  so this is a consistency check, not proof that real LUT and routing
  delays share one temperature coefficient - that needs hardware.

---- PART B : combiner, core 2 five times noisier, 200 CRPs -----------
  combiner     mean |error|   worst |error|   pass rate (d=7)
  mean            2.87            11           96.0 %
  median          3.30            23           92.0 %
  noisy core 2 alone: mean |error| 8.39 LSB

  why the median fails (16 failures):
    noisy core 2 was the median in both measurements : 9
    noisy core 2 was the median in one of them        : 6
    core 2 not the median in either                   : 1
  core 2 is the enrolment median in 54/200 CRPs; switches overall: 48

  NEGATIVE RESULT. Median-of-3 is continuous and never moves more than
  the worst core (checked on every CRP above), so a core switch is not
  a jump. It fails because whenever the noisy core is the middle value -
  at enrolment, at re-measurement, or both - its error goes through at
  FULL weight, while the mean always divides it by 3. A median only
  rejects a bad input when the other inputs agree with each other
  (replicas of one quantity); independent PUF cores do not.

